1. A CLASS WITH NEITHER METHOD: IDENTITY ANSWERS BOTH
     class Point:
         def __init__(self, x, y):
             self.x, self.y = x, y
     p, q = Point(1, 2), Point(1, 2)
     p == q                             False          no __eq__, so == falls back to is
     len({p, q})                        2              and each hashes by identity

2. DEFINE __eq__, AND __hash__ IS GONE
     class Point:
         def __init__(self, x, y):
             self.x, self.y = x, y
         def __eq__(self, other):
             return (self.x, self.y) == (other.x, other.y)
     p, q = Point(1, 2), Point(1, 2)
     p == q                             True
     Point.__hash__                     None           defining __eq__ set __hash__ to None
     hash(p)                            TypeError
     {p, q}                             TypeError      unhashable: no set member, no dict key

3. WHY: EQUAL OBJECTS MUST HASH EQUAL
     class Bad(Point):
         __hash__ = object.__hash__
     p, q = Bad(1, 2), Bad(1, 2)
     p == q                             True
     len({p, q})                        2              equal, yet two members: the contract is broken
     q in {p}                           False          a lookup for q looks where q's hash points

4. THE FIX: HASH THE SAME FIELDS == COMPARES
     class Point:
         def __init__(self, x, y):
             self.x, self.y = x, y
         def __eq__(self, other):
             return (self.x, self.y) == (other.x, other.y)
         def __hash__(self):
             return hash((self.x, self.y))
     p, q = Point(1, 2), Point(1, 2)
     len({p, q})                        1
     q in {p}                           True
     {p: 'here'}[q]                     'here'         q finds p's entry: equal, and hashed alike

5. CHANGE A KEY'S FIELDS, AND THE SET LOSES IT
     s = {p}
     p.x = 99
     p in s                             False          the set looks where the new hash points; p is not there
     p in list(s)                       True           a list uses ==, and finds it
     p.x = 1
     p in s                             True           put the field back, and it is found again

6. WHAT @dataclass DECIDES FOR YOU
     @dataclass
     class D:
         x: int
         y: int
     D(1, 2) == D(1, 2)                 True
     D.__hash__                         None           eq=True and not frozen: unhashable, on purpose
     @dataclass(frozen=True)
     class F:
         x: int
         y: int
     len({F(1, 2), F(1, 2)})            1              frozen: the fields cannot change, so hashing them is safe
     @dataclass(eq=False)
     class I:
         x: int
     I(1) == I(1)                       False          eq=False keeps identity for both
     I.__hash__ is object.__hash__      True

7. == IS TWO QUESTIONS, AND NotImplemented PASSES THE FIRST
     class Metres:
         def __init__(self, v):
             self.v = v
         def __eq__(self, other):
             if not isinstance(other, Metres):
                 return NotImplemented
             return self.v == other.v
     Metres(1) == Metres(1)             True
     Metres(1) != Metres(1)             False          __ne__ is derived from __eq__
     Metres(1) == 1                     False          NotImplemented from both sides: falls back to is
     1 == Metres(1)                     False          int declines first, then Metres is asked
